Question #8:

In the vectors-recycling chunk, z has 6 elements and short has only 2. R still produced an answer instead of an error.

Explain, in your own words, what R’s “recycling rule” did to short to make the addition work.

ANSWER: R's recycling rule repeats the elements of the shorter vector until they match the length of the longer vector. In this example, R repeats 1, 0 three times to match the six elements of z, allowing the addition to work.



Question #9: Now try adding z (length 6) to c(1, 0, 1) (length 3) instead. Does it work cleanly?

ANSWER: Yes, it works cleanly without a warning because R repeats the shorter vector twice to match the six elements of z.



Then try adding z to c(1, 0, 1, 1) (length 4). Compare the warning (or lack of one) in each case, and explain what condition determines whether R recycles silently versus with a warning.

ANSWER: When adding z to c(1, 0, 1, 1), R still produces a result but displays a warning because the length of the longer vector (6) is not an exact multiple of the shorter vector's length (4). R recycles silently when the longer vector's length is divisible by the shorter vector's length; otherwise, it displays a warning.


