Question #10:

student["grades"] and student[["grades"]] look similar but return objects of different classes.

Run class() on each and explain the difference in what single-bracket [ ] versus double-bracket [[ ]] return from a list.

ANSWER:Single brackets [ ] return a list containing the selected element, while double brackets [[ ]] return the actual value or object inside the list. When I use class(), single brackets return "list", while double brackets return "numeric" because the grades element contains numerical values.



Question #11:

Why do you think R makes a distinction between [ ] and [[ ]] for lists, but a vector only needs [ ]? Think about what a list can contain that a vector cannot.

ANSWER:R uses [ ] and [[ ]] for lists because lists can contain different data types, such as text, numbers, logical values, and vectors. Single brackets allow me to select an element while keeping it as a list, whereas double brackets allow me to access the actual contents of that element. Vectors only contain elements of the same basic type, so regular indexing with [ ] is enough to access their elements.


